1,2 $ Pileipellis  with  gelatinous  layer,  easily  separable  when  fresh ;  spores alway s completely smooth, ail hyphae without clamp connections
1,4 $ Pileipellis not separable
2,0 $ Pileus  without  ferruginous  scales;  pigment  of hyphae  of epicutis  yel- lowish  incrusted at most (never brown) $ Crepidotus mollis
2,3 $ Pileus  with yellow-brown  to ferruginous scales; pigment of hypha e of epicutis firmly ferruginous incrusted
3,0 $ Spores 6.5-9 x 4.5-6 pm , mean 6.9-7.8 x 5.0-5.5  pm $ Crepidotus calolepis var. calolepis
3,0 $ Spores 7.5-11  x 6.5-7.5 pm , mean 8.7-10.1  x 5.7-6.6 pm $ Crepidotus calolepis  var. squamulosus
4,5 $ Pileus  coloured  (yellow,  orange,  ferruginous,  brown,  pink  or  red)  or with coloured scales
4,12 $ Pileus white (pale yellow species are included here once again)
5,6 $ Pileus cream to yellowish, sometimes fading to whitish
5,7 $ Pileus  orange-yellow,  reddish  orange,  brown-orange,  pinkish  brown. pink or red
6,0 $ Spores elongate ellipsoidal,  subcylindric,  finely  rugose-rough,  7-10  * 4-5.5  pm ,  Q =  1.7-2.2 $ Crepidotus luteolus
6,0 $ Spores spherical, 4.5-6 (im, with coarse,  irregular acute warts,  mean 5.3-5.4 pm, Q =  1.0-1.1 a $ Crepidotus carpaticus
7,8 $ Spores spherical to  subspherical (Q =  1.0-1.1);  pileus orange-yellow to reddish orange
7,10 $ Spores subglobose to ellipsoidal (Q =  1.2-1.3); pileus somehow pink. brownish pink or red
8,0 $ Pileus covered by  ferruginous-yellow to  ferruginous  scales,  not  hy- grophanous; lamellae young orange, salmon-coloured $ Crepidotus crocophyllus
8,9 $ Pileus smooth, at  most young slightly appressed felty-fibrillous, not scaly
9,0 $ Pileus 5-15 mm wide, vividly orange to carrot-yellow, not hygropha- nous; pileipellis made up of shortly articulate, often septate, 6-11  pm wide  hyphae  (see  HAUSKNECH T &  K.RISA I 1988:  186);  as  yet  only known from Ukraine (Krim) (MOSE R 1993) $ Crepidotus sinuosus
9,0 $ Pileus  15-55 mm wide, pale orange to melon-yellow, hygrophanous; pileipellis made up of long, 4-7 pm wide hyphae $ Crepidotus ehren dorferi 
9,0,n$ (if pileus dirty pale brownish, lamellae without orange tinge, spores in average under 6  pm and cheilocystidia thick, clavate to slightly capi- tate, see brownish forms of Crepidotus applanatus)
10,0 $ Pileus bright red, lamellar edge red underlincd; hyphae without clamp connections; not yet known from Austria $ Crepidotus cinnabarinus
10,11 $ Pileus brownish pink to pink, lamellar edge concolorous; hyphae with clamp connections
11,0 $ Entire  fruitbody  pink,  pileus  felty,  up  to  10  mm  wide;  basidia  4- spored,  cheilocystidia  clavate  with  many  digitaliform excrescences; only known from the type locality in Italy $ Crepidotus roseoornatus
11,0 $ Pileus brownish pink, brick reddish, felty or fibrillose, up to 22 mm wide; basidia  1-, 2-, 3-, 4-spored, cheilocystidia clavate, elongate la- genifonn, sometimes forked apically; France, Belgium $ Crepidotus brunneoroseus
12,13 $ Hyphae without clamp connections
12,14 $ Hyphae with clamp connections
13,0 $ Spores cylindric, 2.5-3.5  pm wide, under light microscope smooth, Q = 2-3; cheilocystidia with long, filiform excrescences $ Crepidotus epibryus
13,0 $ Spores elongate ellipsoidal to cylindric, 4-7 (.un wide, in light micro- scope  nearly smooth to  fïnely rough. 0 = 1.7-2.2; cheilocystidia cyl- indric, often septate $ Crepidotus versutus
14,15 $ Spores  regularly  spherical  (Q  =  1.0-1.1)  with  acute  (not spines; pileus hygrophanous truncate)
14,18 $ Spores  widely  spherical-ellipsoidal, ellipsoidal,  cylindric, smooth to verrucose-spinulose fusiform,
15,0 $ Pileocystidia numerous, widely capitate; spores 5.4-6.3  x  5.2-6.4 pm; as yet only known from Ukraine $ Crepidotus malachioides
15,16 $ Pileocystidis  numerous or rare, cylindric to  slightly fusiform, never capitate
16,0 $ Spores 4.5-6 x 4.5-5.5 pm , spinulose; cheilocystidia widely clavate to capitate $ Crepidotus applunatus
16,17 $ Spores a bit larger (longer and wider); cheilocystidia cylindric-clavate or slenderly cylindric-lageniform
17,0 $ Spores 6-8.5 x 5.5-7.5 ^n , mean 6.4-6.8 x 6.3-6.4 pm; cheilocystidia thick, cylindric to clavate; pileocystidia rare; on deciduous wood $ Crepidotus malachius  var. malachius
17,0 $ Spores 5.5-6.5  x 5-6 pm, mean 6.1  x 5.6 cheilocystidia slender, cylindric-lageniform. pileocystidia many; on coniferous wood $ Crepidotus malachius  var.  tricltifer
18,19 $ Spores under light microscope smooth to indistinctly rough
18,21 $ Spores under light microscope distinctly verrucose to spinulose
19,0 $ Spores completely smooth, thick-walled, never collapsing, ellipsoidal to elongate ellipsoidal, 5-8 x 4-6 pm $ Crépidotus autochthonus
19,20 $ Spores  smooth  to  indistinctly  rough,  thin-walled,  partly  collapsing, ellipsoidal to slightly amygdaliform
20,0 $ Spores 6.5-9  x  3.5-4.5  pm, mean 7.6  x  3.9  ^m, Q =  1.6-2.1, under light microscope completely smooth; cheilocystidia 30-70 pm long, variable, cylindric, cylindric-ventricose, sometimes with latéral ex- crescences, apex mostly obtuse; growing on Cantharellus $ Crepidotus fungiphilus
20,0 $ Spores  7-9  x 4.5-6  pm,  Q  =  1.3-1.8,  under  light  microscope never  completely  smooth,  but  slightly  uneven-rough;  cheilocystidia  30-50 pm  long,  clavate,  capitate,  cylindric,  slightly  lagenifonn ;  on  wood  or plant  débris $ Crepidotus caspari
21,22 $ Spores  spherical  to wide ellipsoidal  (Q =  1.1-1.5),  spinulose or verrucose
21,23 $ Spores  ellipsoidal,  elongate  ellipsoidal,  cylindric  (Q  =  1.5-2),  verru cose, crestate-verrucose
22,0 $ Fruitbody  small,  5-30  mm,  lamellae  whitish to brownish  pink;  spores spinulose $ Crepidotus cesatii
22,0 $ Fruitbody large, 20-70 mm, lamellae soon bright orange; spores coarsely verrucose; not yet known from Austria $ Crepidotus macedonicus
23,0 $ Spores  in  average  over  7.5  x  4.5  pm,  Q  =  1.4-1.7,  long  ellipsoidal, ornament bulging -verrucose; cheilocystidia  slenderly lageniform  to fusifor m with tapering apex $ Crepidotus subverrucisporus (if spores  in  comparison  to  length  nanower ,  Q =  1.8-2.2,  ellipsoidal- subcylindric, and cheilocystidia cylindric, with ramified apex, see Crepidotus luteolus,  bleached  fruitbodies)
23,24 $ Spores in average under 7 x  4 pm, cylindric to ellipsoidal-cylindric
24,0 $ Spores very small, up to 6.5 x 4 jj m , cylindric; cheilocystidia 6-11  pm wide, clavate, lagenifonn, duck-head-like $ Crepidotus variahilis
24,0 $ Spores  larger,  6.2-7.7  x  3.7-4.6  pm,  ellipsoidal  to  cylindric-ellipsoi- dal; cheilocystidia  5-7  pm wide, filiform, narrowly  lagenifonn , some- times ramified; not yet known from Austria $ Crepidotus neotrichocystis
